## Acceptance Criteria

### Core Type System

- [ ] Type lattice implemented for Chalk subset (Any, Scalar, List, Code, None)
- [ ] Scalar subtypes: Undef, Boolean, Str, Num, Int, Ref
- [ ] Ref subtypes: Object, ScalarRef, ArrayRef, HashRef, CodeRef
- [ ] List subtypes: Array, Hash
- [ ] Correct subtyping: Int <: Num <: Str <: Scalar <: Any
- [ ] Ephemeral List type with conversion logic

### Type Membership

- [ ] Syntactic preservation (round-trip coercion) test
- [ ] Semantic fulfillment (operational contracts) test
- [ ] Both criteria required for membership
- [ ] Special cases: NaN, primitive boolean

### Coercions

- [ ] Numeric coercion (⇓^Num) matching spec
- [ ] String coercion (⇓^Str) matching spec
- [ ] Boolean coercion (⇓^Bool) matching spec
- [ ] Warnings on information-losing coercions

### Integration

- [ ] SemanticElement carries both IR nodes and types
- [ ] Type inference during init_element_from_rule
- [ ] Type checking during multiply/add
- [ ] Ephemeral List → Array/Hash conversion tracking

### Testing

- [ ] Unit tests for all type classes
- [ ] Round-trip preservation tests
- [ ] Semantic contract tests
- [ ] Coercion correctness tests
- [ ] Ephemeral List conversion tests
- [ ] Integration tests with SeaOfNodes IR

### Documentation

- [ ] Type lattice fully documented (Chalk subset)
- [ ] Formal membership criteria explained
- [ ] Coercion rules with examples
- [ ] Special cases (NaN, etc.)
- [ ] Ephemeral types explained

## Implementation Phases

### Phase 1: Foundation (Week 1)
- Implement complete type class hierarchy (Chalk subset)
- Round-trip preservation logic
- Semantic contract validation
- Basic test framework

### Phase 2: Coercions (Week 2)
- Implement all coercion rules (⇓^Num, ⇓^Str, ⇓^Bool)
- Coercion warnings
- Type compatibility checking
- Observational equivalence

### Phase 3: Ephemeral Types (Week 3)
- List type implementation
- List → Array/Hash conversion
- Range operator
- Subroutine parameter/return handling

### Phase 4: Integration (Week 4)
- Integrate with semantic semiring (issue #71)
- Type environment tracking
- Type checking during IR composition
- Built-in function signatures

### Phase 5: Polish (Week 5)
- Error message quality
- Documentation
- Comprehensive tests
- Performance optimization

## Dependencies

- Requires: chalk.bnf grammar (issue #67)
- Requires: IR generation (issue #71)
- Builds on: Sea of Nodes IR foundation (PR #73)

## Resources

### Primary Reference
- **Formal specification:** https://gist.github.com/perigrin/c4780a7511ba1421e49a4a8b385aaa3d

### Perl Documentation
- perldata - Perl data types
- perlop - Operators and precedence
- perlfunc - Built-in functions
- perlsub - Subroutine semantics

## Common Pitfalls

1. **Num <: Str ordering:** Remember that Num is a SUBTYPE of Str (counterintuitive but correct per round-trip preservation)
2. **Both criteria required:** Type membership needs BOTH syntactic preservation AND semantic fulfillment
3. **NaN edge case:** "NaN" fails semantic contracts despite syntactic preservation
4. **Boolean vs primitive bool:** Boolean type vs. is_bool() predicate distinction
5. **Ephemeral List:** Exists at runtime but converts on assignment
6. **Information-losing coercions:** Warn when "hello" → 0 or similar
7. **Chalk subset:** No Glob, GlobRef, or DualVar support (not in Chalk's Perl subset)

## Future Enhancements

- Optional explicit type annotations
- Generic/parameterized types
- Union types for precision
- Refinement types with constraints
- Type-directed optimization

